  "You now understand India's federal structure — the three lists, special provisions, and how the Constitution protects this power-sharing arrangement.": "audio/en/c4f47e091c762c9d.mp3",
  "Explore the constitutional design that divides power in the world's largest democracy — the three lists, special provisions, and the role of the judiciary.": "audio/en/c68acbd766cdb5bc.mp3",
  "A third tier of federalism was added in the form of Panchayats (rural local bodies) and Municipalities (urban local bodies) to deepen democracy at the grassroots level.": "audio/en/6be0f3c8760ef650.mp3",
  "Subjects not in any of the three lists fall under residuary powers, which are held by the Union Government. This ensures the central government can address new and emerging issues.": "audio/en/0569c8812240ea90.mp3",
  "The third tier of Indian federalism is Panchayats (rural) and Municipalities (urban) — local government bodies that were given constitutional status to deepen democracy at the grassroots level.": "audio/en/3e40823704ecee46.mp3",
  "Congratulations! You have completed this section. You now understand India's federal structure, the three lists, special provisions, and how the Constitution protects this power-sharing arrangement.": "audio/en/c2c9f6c60180c2b1.mp3",
  "For subjects not in any list — like computer software — the Union government has the power to legislate. These are called residuary subjects. This three-list system ensures clear boundaries while allowing flexibility for new challenges.": "audio/en/535e065c6f8be7e8.mp3",
  "Each level of government needs its own revenue sources to ensure financial autonomy. Without independent funding, a level of government would be dependent on the other and could not truly function as an independent tier of the federation.": "audio/en/0ca02d6bb6716ff5.mp3",
  "Welcome to the section — What Makes India Federal? In this section, we explore the constitutional design that divides power in the world's largest democracy — the three lists, special provisions, and the role of the judiciary. Press Begin Learning when you are ready.": "audio/en/3331ce50ec002091.mp3",
  "The Constitution originally provided for two tiers — the Union Government and State Governments. Later, a third tier was added in the form of Panchayats and Municipalities, giving India a three-level federal structure. Union Territories like Delhi, Chandigarh, and Lakshadweep have a special arrangement where the central government has more direct control.": "audio/en/9accdf07dc8f131f.mp3",
  "The Indian Constitution provides a three-fold distribution of legislative powers through three lists. The Union List includes subjects of national importance like defence, foreign affairs, banking, and currency — only the Union government can make laws on these. The State List covers subjects like police, trade, agriculture, and irrigation — only states can legislate here.": "audio/en/13ccdac628e692c9.mp3",
  "Each tier of government is constitutionally empowered to levy specific taxes. This financial autonomy means that neither level depends entirely on the other for funds, preserving the federal balance.": "audio/en/b8b7ad3e55ac92da.mp3",
  "Both levels of government have the power to raise resources by levying taxes. The Union Government collects income tax, customs duty, and central GST, while State Governments collect state GST, taxes on agricultural income, stamp duty, and vehicle tax. This financial autonomy is essential — if one level depended on the other for funds, it could not function independently.": "audio/en/b4c5ae0d6c1e297b.mp3",
  "The Indian Constitution provides a three-fold distribution of legislative powers: the Union List (national importance), the State List (state and local importance), and the Concurrent List (both). In case of conflict on Concurrent subjects, Union law prevails.": "audio/en/cc3f26c867ab3f41.mp3",
  "Not all states have equal powers. States like Assam, Nagaland, Arunachal Pradesh, and Mizoram enjoy special powers under Article 371 due to their unique social and historical circumstances. These include protection of indigenous land rights, culture, and preferential employment in government service. Some areas are too small to be states but cannot merge with existing ones — these become Union Territories.": "audio/en/8a9ff0e827267e8f.mp3",
  "Not all states have equal powers: some enjoy special powers under Article 371, while tiny territories become Union Territories, where the Centre has special powers. Amendments to this system require at least a two-thirds majority in Parliament and ratification by at least half the states.": "audio/en/828d287ad5e72c31.mp3",
  "The power-sharing arrangement is protected by the Constitution. Parliament alone cannot change it — any amendment needs at least a two-thirds majority in both Houses, plus ratification by at least half the state legislatures. The judiciary — High Courts and Supreme Court — settles disputes between levels of government.": "audio/en/97786b764e835e76.mp3",
  "Changing the federal power-sharing arrangement requires at least a two-thirds majority in both Houses of Parliament AND ratification by the legislatures of at least half of the total States.": "audio/en/7712276cac3a557a.mp3"
